new(121,"PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G2:U08:L01","https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-2/unit-8/lesson-1/preparation.html","1ddbcdadb7bf76ed1b9b1807dab711656c98756f0fbdf2d902f9701aa40901cd",new string[]{"CCSS:2.OA.C.3"},new string[]{"Learning goal: You will arrange a number of objects into 2 equal groups and learn that some numbers of objects can be put into two equal groups without any objects left over.\nIn this lesson, you learn that some numbers of objects can be split into two equal groups, without any objects left over, and other numbers cannot. The work of this lesson builds on your real-world experiences with equal sharing and prepares them to understand and use the terms even and odd to describe groups of objects in future lessons (MP6). In the first activity, you separate objects into 2 equal groups and begin to create a list of numbers that can be split into 2 equal groups.","Key ideas:\n- Determine whether a group of objects can be arranged into 2 equal groups\n- In this lesson, you learn that some numbers of objects can be split into two equal groups, without any objects left over, and other numbers cannot\n- Compare actual values or measured attributes, not the visual size or position of a drawing.","Example 1: Choose a container of counters. Share the counters equally with your partner. Then complete the table to show the total number of objects and how many you each received. total my share my partner’s share number of leftovers\nApproach: “Look at the numbers in each group. What do you notice? What do you wonder?” (I notice all the numbers show two equal groups. I notice there’s only ever one leftover. I notice some numbers have the same equal groups, like 6 has 2 groups of 3 and 7 has 2 groups of 3. I wonder why there’s only ever one leftover.)\nExample 2: Andre has a collection of 17 marbles. He wants to play a game with his sister. They both need to start with the same number of marbles and they want to use as many as they can. How many marbles would Andre and his sister get? Would there be any marbles left out of the game? Show your thinking. What if Andre had 18 marbles? How many would each person get?...\nApproach: “How did you use your work for sharing 18 marbles to find out how to share 20 marbles?” (20 is just 2 more than 18 so each group would get 1. There wouldn’t be any left over.) “What equation can we write to represent the diagram?” ( \\(10 + 10 = 20\\) )","Step 1: Identify the quantities or expressions to compare.\nStep 2: Represent or calculate each value accurately.\nStep 3: Decide whether one is greater, less, or equal.\nStep 4: Write or explain the comparison.\nStep 5: Recheck both values before finalizing.","Common mistake: deciding from appearance instead of actual values. Represent or calculate both quantities before comparing.","You will arrange a number of objects into 2 equal groups and learn that some numbers of objects can be put into two equal groups without any objects left over. In this lesson, you learn that some numbers of objects can be split into two equal groups, without any objects left over, and other numbers cannot."},new string[]{"IM2021 G2 Unit8 Lesson1 Can You Share?. Actual original publisher activity: Equal sharing original containers with 4-15 counters; split in 2 equal shares, record each share plus 0/1 leftover, trade containers, original class T-chart. OPEN source boundaries: Original toy image and real randomized counter containers/student T-chart human.","Authentic lesson-specific goal: Equal sharing original containers with 4-15 counters; split in 2 equal shares, record each share plus 0/1 leftover, trade containers, original class T-chart. Source CCSS references CCSS:2.OA.C.3. FIRST-PARTY numerical practice is independently authored, not a publisher assessment.","Authentic student-facing activity, with source number constraints and teacher-only drawings: Equal sharing original containers with 4-15 counters; split in 2 equal shares, record each share plus 0/1 leftover, trade containers, original class T-chart. In Edulytics, use newly constructed numerical cases and disclose independent origin. Original toy image and real randomized counter containers/student T-chart human.","Step1 identify actual publisher objective. Step2 show original with real counters, pair shares, equal addends, array or physical equal-size square tile. Step3 for independent first-party practice count equal groups, parity, repeated addition, or array size precisely. Step4 confirm residuals or row/column sums, with no guessing missing source card/picture values. Step5 do not automatically certify student physical representation or oral explanation. Equal sharing original containers with 4-15 counters; split in 2 equal shares, record each share plus 0/1 leftover, trade containers, original class T-chart.","Likely error: classify parity without checking leftover, confuse equal groups with arbitrary dots, call nonrectangular spacing an array, or use unequal-size partition tiles. Original toy image and real randomized counter containers/student T-chart human.","Grade2 Unit8 Lesson1. Source: Equal sharing original containers with 4-15 counters; split in 2 equal shares, record each share plus 0/1 leftover, trade containers, original class T-chart. Human boundary: Original toy image and real randomized counter containers/student T-chart human."}),
new(122,"PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G2:U08:L02","https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-2/unit-8/lesson-2/preparation.html","660fafd839d002c655c9495a460b037892e07cea3cc2a907d0bd7ba17246a28a",new string[]{"CCSS:2.OA.C.3"},new string[]{"Learning goal: You will pair all of the objects in a group and understand that some numbers of objects can be paired without any objects left over.\nIn a previous lesson, you determined whether a group of objects could be split into 2 equal groups and discovered that for some numbers of objects, there will be 1 leftover. In this lesson, you learn that some numbers of objects can be split into multiple groups of 2 (pairs) without any incomplete pairs. In the lesson synthesis, you compare the charts from this lesson and the previous lesson that show no leftovers or one leftover.","Key ideas:\n- Determine if a group of objects can be arranged into groups of 2\n- In a previous lesson, you determined whether a group of objects could be split into 2 equal groups and discovered that for some numbers of objects, there will be 1 leftover\n- Compare actual values or measured attributes, not the visual size or position of a drawing.","Example 1: Which one doesn’t belong? A Caption: A B Caption: B C Caption: C D Caption: D\nApproach: “How does each image show pairs of socks?” (Sample responses: A shows 4 pairs side-by-side, but you don’t match. B shows 3 pairs of socks that are the same color and right next to each other. C shows socks in 2 rows. The matching pairs are above and below each other. D shows matching pairs side-by-side.)\nExample 2: Make pairs with 1 yellow counter and 1 red counter. my counters total counters leftover counters my counters total counters leftover counters\nApproach: “How did you count the counters to find the total?” (I counted each pair by 2.) “Look at the numbers in each group. What do you notice? What do you wonder?” (All of the numbers show pairs. The numbers in the leftover group have only 1 leftover, the rest make pairs. I wonder what other numbers go in these groups. I wonder if these are the same numbers we found had 1 leftover yesterday.)","Step 1: Identify what the task asks you to find or show.\nStep 2: Represent the important quantities or relationships.\nStep 3: Apply the key idea from the lesson.\nStep 4: Explain how the representation supports the result.\nStep 5: Check that the result matches the question.","Common mistake: jumping to an answer before checking that the representation matches the quantities and relationship in the task.","You will pair all of the objects in a group and understand that some numbers of objects can be paired without any objects left over. In a previous lesson, you determined whether a group of objects could be split into 2 equal groups and discovered that for some numbers of objects, there will be 1 leftover."},new string[]{"IM2021 G2 Unit8 Lesson2 Partners Make Pairs. Actual original publisher activity: Form real red/yellow pairs 4-15 counters, leftovers; class sizes 18,20 have pairs, 19 one leftover; original sock figures and chart. OPEN source boundaries: Original sock visuals, bag arrangement, color pairs, true partner behavior human.","Authentic lesson-specific goal: Form real red/yellow pairs 4-15 counters, leftovers; class sizes 18,20 have pairs, 19 one leftover; original sock figures and chart. Source CCSS references CCSS:2.OA.C.3. FIRST-PARTY numerical practice is independently authored, not a publisher assessment.","Authentic student-facing activity, with source number constraints and teacher-only drawings: Form real red/yellow pairs 4-15 counters, leftovers; class sizes 18,20 have pairs, 19 one leftover; original sock figures and chart. In Edulytics, use newly constructed numerical cases and disclose independent origin. Original sock visuals, bag arrangement, color pairs, true partner behavior human.","Step1 identify actual publisher objective. Step2 show original with real counters, pair shares, equal addends, array or physical equal-size square tile. Step3 for independent first-party practice count equal groups, parity, repeated addition, or array size precisely. Step4 confirm residuals or row/column sums, with no guessing missing source card/picture values. Step5 do not automatically certify student physical representation or oral explanation. Form real red/yellow pairs 4-15 counters, leftovers; class sizes 18,20 have pairs, 19 one leftover; original sock figures and chart.","Likely error: classify parity without checking leftover, confuse equal groups with arbitrary dots, call nonrectangular spacing an array, or use unequal-size partition tiles. Original sock visuals, bag arrangement, color pairs, true partner behavior human.","Grade2 Unit8 Lesson2. Source: Form real red/yellow pairs 4-15 counters, leftovers; class sizes 18,20 have pairs, 19 one leftover; original sock figures and chart. Human boundary: Original sock visuals, bag arrangement, color pairs, true partner behavior human."}),
new(123,"PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G2:U08:L03","https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-2/unit-8/lesson-3/preparation.html","41ca33c004b6c33458ca2f0e9003ccfc115f9077702d9c57ac358b6ece291f9c",new string[]{"CCSS:2.OA.C.3"},new string[]{"Learning goal: You will determine whether representations of groups of objects show an even or odd number of objects.\nIn a previous lesson, you learned the terms even and odd and saw that if a group has an even number of objects, it can be separated into two equal groups and that each object can be paired with another object. In this lesson, you justify why a number is even or odd using methods based on making two equal groups, pairing objects, or skip-counting by 2. Some you may begin to justify why a group of objects has an even or odd number of members by using equations with two equal addends to represent even numbers of objects.","Key ideas:\n- Determine whether representations of groups of objects show an even or odd number of objects\n- In a previous lesson, you learned the terms even and odd and saw that if a group has an even number of objects, it can be separated into two equal groups and that each object can be paired with another object\n- Compare actual values or measured attributes, not the visual size or position of a drawing.","Example 1: Han wants to color a design with the same number of yellow and blue shapes. A Caption: A B Caption: B C Caption: C D Caption: D Which designs could Han choose? Show your thinking using diagrams, symbols, or other representations. Use counters if it helps. Draw a circle design that would work for Han and a circle design that would not work. Priya drew a...\nApproach: Represent the quantities or relationships clearly, apply the lesson idea, and check that the result answers the question.\nExample 2: Sort your cards into a group that shows an even number and a group that shows an odd number. Explain your thinking to your partner. Find 3 cards that show the same number. Explain to your partner how each card shows whether the number is even or odd in a different way.\nApproach: “Which designs have an even number of shapes? Explain how you know.” (A and D because there is an equal number of yellow and blue. A and D because every yellow circle can be paired with a blue circle.) “Does Priya’s design have an even or odd number of shapes? Explain how you know.” (Even. You could make 2 equal groups of shapes. You could pair each circle with another shape.)","Step 1: Identify what the task asks you to find or show.\nStep 2: Represent the important quantities or relationships.\nStep 3: Apply the key idea from the lesson.\nStep 4: Explain how the representation supports the result.\nStep 5: Check that the result matches the question.","Common mistake: jumping to an answer before checking that the representation matches the quantities and relationship in the task.","You will determine whether representations of groups of objects show an even or odd number of objects. In a previous lesson, you learned the terms even and odd and saw that if a group has an even number of objects, it can be separated into two equal groups and that each object can be paired with another object."},new string[]{"IM2021 G2 Unit8 Lesson3 Is it Odd or Even? PLC Activity. Actual original publisher activity: Color by Number original figure collections, color blue/yellow pairs or equal groups and classify even/odd within20. OPEN source boundaries: Publisher color-by-number image and actual colored responses human.","Authentic lesson-specific goal: Color by Number original figure collections, color blue/yellow pairs or equal groups and classify even/odd within20. Source CCSS references CCSS:2.OA.C.3. FIRST-PARTY numerical practice is independently authored, not a publisher assessment.","Authentic student-facing activity, with source number constraints and teacher-only drawings: Color by Number original figure collections, color blue/yellow pairs or equal groups and classify even/odd within20. In Edulytics, use newly constructed numerical cases and disclose independent origin. Publisher color-by-number image and actual colored responses human.","Step1 identify actual publisher objective. Step2 show original with real counters, pair shares, equal addends, array or physical equal-size square tile. Step3 for independent first-party practice count equal groups, parity, repeated addition, or array size precisely. Step4 confirm residuals or row/column sums, with no guessing missing source card/picture values. Step5 do not automatically certify student physical representation or oral explanation. Color by Number original figure collections, color blue/yellow pairs or equal groups and classify even/odd within20.","Likely error: classify parity without checking leftover, confuse equal groups with arbitrary dots, call nonrectangular spacing an array, or use unequal-size partition tiles. Publisher color-by-number image and actual colored responses human.","Grade2 Unit8 Lesson3. Source: Color by Number original figure collections, color blue/yellow pairs or equal groups and classify even/odd within20. Human boundary: Publisher color-by-number image and actual colored responses human."}),
new(124,"PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G2:U08:L04","https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-2/unit-8/lesson-4/preparation.html","44e41667d1493f8c8c0c7fc42233ac5bef3e504f97e817eff0fbe2f47a13cbae",new string[]{"CCSS:2.OA.B.2","CCSS:2.OA.C.3"},new string[]{"Learning goal: You will represent even numbers as the sum of two equal addends.\nIn previous lessons, you determined whether groups of objects and representations had an even or odd number of objects by creating 2 equal groups or pairing objects. In this lesson, you explore patterns in the ways you can represent even and odd numbers as sums of two addends. In the first activity, you decompose even and odd numbers in different ways and notice that only the even numbers of objects could be decomposed into two equal addends.","Key ideas:\n- Represent an even number as the sum of two equal addends\n- In previous lessons, you determined whether groups of objects and representations had an even or odd number of objects by creating 2 equal groups or pairing objects\n- The addends are the quantities being combined and the sum is the resulting total; the expression must match the representation.","Example 1: Find the value of each expression mentally. \\(6 + 6\\) \\(7 + 7\\) \\(7 + 8\\) \\(8 + 9\\)\nApproach: Represent the quantities, write a matching addition relationship, then combine or count on to find and check the total.\nExample 2: Kiran baked 12 cookies. He wants to put them in two gift bags. Show a few different ways he can share the cookies. Can both bags have the same amount of cookies? \\(12 = \\underline{\\hspace{1 cm}} + \\underline{\\hspace{1 cm}}\\) Can both bags have an even number of cookies? \\(12 = \\underline{\\hspace{1 cm}} + \\underline{\\hspace{1 cm}}\\) Can both bags have an...\nApproach: \\(12 = 6 + 6\\) \\(14 = 7 + 7\\) \\(15 = 7 + 8\\) “Which you baked an even number of cookies? Use the equations to explain how you know.” (Kiran and Lin because the equations show you can split your cookies into two equal groups.)","Step 1: Identify the quantities being combined.\nStep 2: Represent each quantity with objects, a drawing, number line, or expression.\nStep 3: Write an addition relationship that matches the representation.\nStep 4: Find the total using an efficient strategy.\nStep 5: Check that the total matches the original quantities.","Common mistake: counting an item twice or combining the wrong quantities. Check the addends and verify the total.","You will represent even numbers as the sum of two equal addends. In previous lessons, you determined whether groups of objects and representations had an even or odd number of objects by creating 2 equal groups or pairing objects."},new string[]{"IM2021 G2 Unit8 Lesson4 Decompose Even and Odd Numbers. Actual original publisher activity: Decompose even numbers into two equal addends, odd into two near equal addends plus 1; example 8=4+4,7=3+3+1. OPEN source boundaries: Original picture cards and student-written multiple equal-addend equations human.","Authentic lesson-specific goal: Decompose even numbers into two equal addends, odd into two near equal addends plus 1; example 8=4+4,7=3+3+1. Source CCSS references CCSS:2.OA.B.2,CCSS:2.OA.C.3. FIRST-PARTY numerical practice is independently authored, not a publisher assessment.","Authentic student-facing activity, with source number constraints and teacher-only drawings: Decompose even numbers into two equal addends, odd into two near equal addends plus 1; example 8=4+4,7=3+3+1. In Edulytics, use newly constructed numerical cases and disclose independent origin. Original picture cards and student-written multiple equal-addend equations human.","Step1 identify actual publisher objective. Step2 show original with real counters, pair shares, equal addends, array or physical equal-size square tile. Step3 for independent first-party practice count equal groups, parity, repeated addition, or array size precisely. Step4 confirm residuals or row/column sums, with no guessing missing source card/picture values. Step5 do not automatically certify student physical representation or oral explanation. Decompose even numbers into two equal addends, odd into two near equal addends plus 1; example 8=4+4,7=3+3+1.","Likely error: classify parity without checking leftover, confuse equal groups with arbitrary dots, call nonrectangular spacing an array, or use unequal-size partition tiles. Original picture cards and student-written multiple equal-addend equations human.","Grade2 Unit8 Lesson4. Source: Decompose even numbers into two equal addends, odd into two near equal addends plus 1; example 8=4+4,7=3+3+1. Human boundary: Original picture cards and student-written multiple equal-addend equations human."}),
new(125,"PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G2:U08:L05","https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-2/unit-8/lesson-5/preparation.html","079c96edc04f4dfaf7c69c41144d4d589219a6b5532d0435865ecb2cc90e903a",new string[]{"CCSS:2.OA.B.2","CCSS:2.OA.C.3"},new string[]{"Learning goal: You will the purpose of this optional lesson is for you to notice and describe patterns in sums within 20 using what you know about even and odd numbers.\nIn previous lessons, you worked with physical objects and images to determine if groups of objects were even or odd. You represented even numbers as an equation with two equal addends. In this lesson, you continue to practice identifying and justifying even and odd numbers and deepen your number sense with numbers within 20.","Key ideas:\n- Recognize patterns in sums of odd and even numbers\n- In previous lessons, you worked with physical objects and images to determine if groups of objects were even or odd\n- Compare actual values or measured attributes, not the visual size or position of a drawing.","Example 1: How many do you see? How do you see them?\nApproach: “Which images show even groups of dots?” (image 1 and image 3)\nExample 2: In the first column of your recording sheet, decide whether each student has an even or odd number of counters. Show your reasoning and circle your choice. Complete the gray column. Does adding 1 change whether the number of counters is even or odd? Explain. Complete the last column. Does adding 2 change whether the number of counters is even or odd?...\nApproach: \\(16 = 8 + 8\\) \\(17 = 8 + 8 + 1\\) \\(18 = 8 + 8 + 2\\) “How do these equations show that adding 1 changes whether a number is even or odd, but adding 2 does not?” (You can see 2 equal groups of 8 in each. 17 is odd because there is one left out. 18 is even because you have 2 equal groups and 1 pair. You could give each group 1 to make it \\(18 = 9 + 9\\) .)","Step 1: Identify what the task asks you to find or show.\nStep 2: Represent the important quantities or relationships.\nStep 3: Apply the key idea from the lesson.\nStep 4: Explain how the representation supports the result.\nStep 5: Check that the result matches the question.","Common mistake: jumping to an answer before checking that the representation matches the quantities and relationship in the task.","You will the purpose of this optional lesson is for you to notice and describe patterns in sums within 20 using what you know about even and odd numbers. In previous lessons, you worked with physical objects and images to determine if groups of objects were even or odd."},new string[]{"IM2021 G2 Unit8 Lesson5 Patterns with Even and Odd Numbers (optional). Actual original publisher activity: OPTIONAL dot figures 6,13,14, stand/sit count 0..20, investigate +1 reverses parity and +2 preserves parity. OPEN source boundaries: Original dot photos and circle dance, individual counter reasoning and chosen numbers human.","Authentic lesson-specific goal: OPTIONAL dot figures 6,13,14, stand/sit count 0..20, investigate +1 reverses parity and +2 preserves parity. Source CCSS references CCSS:2.OA.B.2,CCSS:2.OA.C.3. FIRST-PARTY numerical practice is independently authored, not a publisher assessment.","Authentic student-facing activity, with source number constraints and teacher-only drawings: OPTIONAL dot figures 6,13,14, stand/sit count 0..20, investigate +1 reverses parity and +2 preserves parity. In Edulytics, use newly constructed numerical cases and disclose independent origin. Original dot photos and circle dance, individual counter reasoning and chosen numbers human.","Step1 identify actual publisher objective. Step2 show original with real counters, pair shares, equal addends, array or physical equal-size square tile. Step3 for independent first-party practice count equal groups, parity, repeated addition, or array size precisely. Step4 confirm residuals or row/column sums, with no guessing missing source card/picture values. Step5 do not automatically certify student physical representation or oral explanation. OPTIONAL dot figures 6,13,14, stand/sit count 0..20, investigate +1 reverses parity and +2 preserves parity.","Likely error: classify parity without checking leftover, confuse equal groups with arbitrary dots, call nonrectangular spacing an array, or use unequal-size partition tiles. Original dot photos and circle dance, individual counter reasoning and chosen numbers human.","Grade2 Unit8 Lesson5. Source: OPTIONAL dot figures 6,13,14, stand/sit count 0..20, investigate +1 reverses parity and +2 preserves parity. Human boundary: Original dot photos and circle dance, individual counter reasoning and chosen numbers human."}),
new(126,"PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G2:U08:L06","https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-2/unit-8/lesson-6/preparation.html","8d9364b14eb31836990d3a40a3c7b39ace0d8c673daac769868da9560221c5da",new string[]{"CCSS:2.NBT.A.2","CCSS:2.NBT.B.7","CCSS:2.NBT.B.8","CCSS:2.OA.B.2"},new string[]{"Learning goal: You will skip-count by 2, 5, and 10 and to add and subtract within 1,000.\nThis lesson is optional because it is an opportunity for extra practice that not all classes may need. In Activity 1, you learn stage 4 of the Write Numbers center, which was first introduced in grade 1. In this new stage, called Skip Count by 2, 5, and 10, you write numbers as you skip-count by 2, 5, or 10.","Key ideas:\n- Add and subtract within 1,000 using strategies based on place value and the properties of operations\n- Skip-count by 2, 5, and 10\n- Subtraction can represent taking away, comparing quantities, or finding a missing addend; the representation must match the situation.","Example 1: Find the value of each expression mentally. \\(2 + 2 + 2\\) \\(2 + 2 + 2 + 2\\) \\(2 + 2 + 2 + 2 + 2\\) \\(2 + 2 + 2 + 2 + 2 + 2\\)\nApproach: “Which expressions could represent an even number of objects? Explain.” (You all could. Each 2 is like a pair. You can find each sum by counting by 2.)","Step 1: Identify the whole and the part being removed or compared.\nStep 2: Represent the situation with objects, a drawing, number line, or equation.\nStep 3: Write a subtraction relationship that matches the representation.\nStep 4: Find the difference using an efficient strategy.\nStep 5: Check the result with the original quantities or a related addition fact.","Common mistake: mixing up the whole and the part being removed or compared. Check that the subtraction relationship matches the representation.","You will skip-count by 2, 5, and 10 and to add and subtract within 1,000. This lesson is optional because it is an opportunity for extra practice that not all classes may need."},new string[]{"IM2021 G2 Unit8 Lesson6 Center Day 1 (optional). Actual original publisher activity: OPTIONAL Write Numbers Stage4: partner chooses skip count step2/5/10, each turn writes next1-3 terms; last gameboard entry wins. OPEN source boundaries: Original board, turns, winner and self-chosen alternate centers human.","Authentic lesson-specific goal: OPTIONAL Write Numbers Stage4: partner chooses skip count step2/5/10, each turn writes next1-3 terms; last gameboard entry wins. Source CCSS references CCSS:2.NBT.A.2,CCSS:2.NBT.B.7,CCSS:2.NBT.B.8,CCSS:2.OA.B.2. FIRST-PARTY numerical practice is independently authored, not a publisher assessment.","Authentic student-facing activity, with source number constraints and teacher-only drawings: OPTIONAL Write Numbers Stage4: partner chooses skip count step2/5/10, each turn writes next1-3 terms; last gameboard entry wins. In Edulytics, use newly constructed numerical cases and disclose independent origin. Original board, turns, winner and self-chosen alternate centers human.","Step1 identify actual publisher objective. Step2 show original with real counters, pair shares, equal addends, array or physical equal-size square tile. Step3 for independent first-party practice count equal groups, parity, repeated addition, or array size precisely. Step4 confirm residuals or row/column sums, with no guessing missing source card/picture values. Step5 do not automatically certify student physical representation or oral explanation. OPTIONAL Write Numbers Stage4: partner chooses skip count step2/5/10, each turn writes next1-3 terms; last gameboard entry wins.","Likely error: classify parity without checking leftover, confuse equal groups with arbitrary dots, call nonrectangular spacing an array, or use unequal-size partition tiles. Original board, turns, winner and self-chosen alternate centers human.","Grade2 Unit8 Lesson6. Source: OPTIONAL Write Numbers Stage4: partner chooses skip count step2/5/10, each turn writes next1-3 terms; last gameboard entry wins. Human boundary: Original board, turns, winner and self-chosen alternate centers human."}),
new(127,"PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G2:U08:L07","https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-2/unit-8/lesson-7/preparation.html","2e4275d29eaa9fd2312771c81e52435a7810bcd34bed33d6b3725962ae7ec4be",new string[]{"CCSS:2.OA.B.2","CCSS:2.OA.C.3","CCSS:2.OA.C.4"},new string[]{"Learning goal: You will learn that an array is an arrangement of objects into rows with an equal number of objects in each row.\nIn the first section of this unit, you determined whether a group of objects was odd or even by organizing them in pairs or putting them into 2 equal groups. In this lesson, you describe the structure of an array. You describe the number of objects in each row of an array and learn that the total number of objects in an array can be determined by finding the sum of the number of objects in each row.","Key ideas:\n- Describe an array as an arrangement of objects into rows with an equal number of objects in each row\n- In the first section of this unit, you determined whether a group of objects was odd or even by organizing them in pairs or putting them into 2 equal groups\n- Measurement units must be equal in size and placed or interpreted without gaps, overlaps, or a shifted starting point.","Example 1: Which one doesn’t belong? A Caption: A B Caption: B C Caption: C D Caption: D\nApproach: “How are the circles in B arranged differently than in the other groups?” (You are all lined up. You are arranged in rows. There are the same number of circles in each row. There are the same number of circles in columns, too.)\nExample 2: Arrange each set of counters into equal rows with no extra counters. How many counters are there in all? ___ Show how you arranged them using words, drawings, or numbers. How many counters are there in all? ___ Show how you arranged them using words, drawings, or numbers. How many counters are there in all? ___ Show how you arranged them using words,...\nApproach: “7 and 9 are both odd numbers. What did you notice about the arrays you could make with them?” (I couldn't make an array with 2 rows. With 9 counters, I could do 3 rows with 3 counters in each row. For 7, the only way we did it was with 1 row with all 7 counters.) “How can we describe this array of 6 counters?” (There are 3 on the top and 3 on the bottom.)","Step 1: Identify the quantities or expressions to compare.\nStep 2: Represent or calculate each value accurately.\nStep 3: Decide whether one is greater, less, or equal.\nStep 4: Write or explain the comparison.\nStep 5: Recheck both values before finalizing.","Common mistake: deciding from appearance instead of actual values. Represent or calculate both quantities before comparing.","You will learn that an array is an arrangement of objects into rows with an equal number of objects in each row. In the first section of this unit, you determined whether a group of objects was odd or even by organizing them in pairs or putting them into 2 equal groups."},new string[]{"IM2021 G2 Unit8 Lesson7 What is an Array?. Actual original publisher activity: Original dot source B is 3rows×4 true array; student arrays with 6,7,9 counters, equal rows and columns, 7 cannot have 2+ rows AND 2+ columns. OPEN source boundaries: Actual source dot layouts and student-built arrays, legitimate different arrangements human.","Authentic lesson-specific goal: Original dot source B is 3rows×4 true array; student arrays with 6,7,9 counters, equal rows and columns, 7 cannot have 2+ rows AND 2+ columns. Source CCSS references CCSS:2.OA.B.2,CCSS:2.OA.C.3,CCSS:2.OA.C.4. FIRST-PARTY numerical practice is independently authored, not a publisher assessment.","Authentic student-facing activity, with source number constraints and teacher-only drawings: Original dot source B is 3rows×4 true array; student arrays with 6,7,9 counters, equal rows and columns, 7 cannot have 2+ rows AND 2+ columns. In Edulytics, use newly constructed numerical cases and disclose independent origin. Actual source dot layouts and student-built arrays, legitimate different arrangements human.","Step1 identify actual publisher objective. Step2 show original with real counters, pair shares, equal addends, array or physical equal-size square tile. Step3 for independent first-party practice count equal groups, parity, repeated addition, or array size precisely. Step4 confirm residuals or row/column sums, with no guessing missing source card/picture values. Step5 do not automatically certify student physical representation or oral explanation. Original dot source B is 3rows×4 true array; student arrays with 6,7,9 counters, equal rows and columns, 7 cannot have 2+ rows AND 2+ columns.","Likely error: classify parity without checking leftover, confuse equal groups with arbitrary dots, call nonrectangular spacing an array, or use unequal-size partition tiles. Actual source dot layouts and student-built arrays, legitimate different arrangements human.","Grade2 Unit8 Lesson7. Source: Original dot source B is 3rows×4 true array; student arrays with 6,7,9 counters, equal rows and columns, 7 cannot have 2+ rows AND 2+ columns. Human boundary: Actual source dot layouts and student-built arrays, legitimate different arrangements human."}),
new(128,"PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G2:U08:L08","https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-2/unit-8/lesson-8/preparation.html","be9f247e9eedf7d2ebbae8cc3636da6283c8bd8d2837aefb48b60a301134a5ad",new string[]{"CCSS:2.OA.B.2","CCSS:2.OA.C.3","CCSS:2.OA.C.4"},new string[]{"Learning goal: You will see that in an array of objects, the objects are arranged into columns with an equal number of objects in each column.\nIn the previous lesson, you learned that an array is an arrangement of objects into rows with an equal number of objects in each row. In this lesson, you refine your understanding of an array to include the fact that the objects in a row are equally spaced from each other. When arranged this way, the objects also line up into columns.","Key ideas:\n- Describe an array as an arrangement of objects into columns with an equal number of objects in each column\n- In the previous lesson, you learned that an array is an arrangement of objects into rows with an equal number of objects in each row\n- Compare actual values or measured attributes, not the visual size or position of a drawing.","Example 1: How many counters do you see? Record an estimate that is: too low about right too high \\(\\phantom{\\hspace{2.5cm} \\\\ \\hspace{2.5cm}}\\) \\(\\phantom{\\hspace{2.5cm} \\\\ \\hspace{2.5cm}}\\) \\(\\phantom{\\hspace{2.5cm} \\\\ \\hspace{2.5cm}}\\)\nApproach: “We saw different arrangements of the same number of counters. Which one makes it easier to tell how many there are altogether? Explain.” (In the last way, it is easy to see it is 10 and another 10. It looks like 2 10-frames.) “Organizing the circles into arrays can help us see ways to find the total more quickly. How could I use skip counting to find the total number of counters?” (We could count by 5 or 2.)\nExample 2: How many columns are in this array? How many counters are in each column? How many counters are there in all? How many columns are in this array? How many counters are in each column? How many counters are there in all? Use 10 counters to make 2 columns with the same number in each column. How many counters are in each column? How many rows are in the...\nApproach: “Describe ___ ’s array.” (It has 10 counters, it has 2 columns and 5 counters in each column. It has 5 rows with 2 counters in each row.) “How can we prove there are 10 counters in this array?” (We can skip count by 2. We can add \\(2 + 2 + 2 + 2 + 2\\) . We can add \\(5 + 5\\) .)","Step 1: Identify the quantities or expressions to compare.\nStep 2: Represent or calculate each value accurately.\nStep 3: Decide whether one is greater, less, or equal.\nStep 4: Write or explain the comparison.\nStep 5: Recheck both values before finalizing.","Common mistake: deciding from appearance instead of actual values. Represent or calculate both quantities before comparing.","You will see that in an array of objects, the objects are arranged into columns with an equal number of objects in each column. In the previous lesson, you learned that an array is an arrangement of objects into rows with an equal number of objects in each row."},new string[]{"IM2021 G2 Unit8 Lesson8 Count Columns and Objects in Columns. Actual original publisher activity: Identify columns as vertical, rows horizontal in authentic source arrays; count total by equal columns and repeated addition. OPEN source boundaries: Original printed source arrays and student rows/columns drawing human.","Authentic lesson-specific goal: Identify columns as vertical, rows horizontal in authentic source arrays; count total by equal columns and repeated addition. Source CCSS references CCSS:2.OA.B.2,CCSS:2.OA.C.3,CCSS:2.OA.C.4. FIRST-PARTY numerical practice is independently authored, not a publisher assessment.","Authentic student-facing activity, with source number constraints and teacher-only drawings: Identify columns as vertical, rows horizontal in authentic source arrays; count total by equal columns and repeated addition. In Edulytics, use newly constructed numerical cases and disclose independent origin. Original printed source arrays and student rows/columns drawing human.","Step1 identify actual publisher objective. Step2 show original with real counters, pair shares, equal addends, array or physical equal-size square tile. Step3 for independent first-party practice count equal groups, parity, repeated addition, or array size precisely. Step4 confirm residuals or row/column sums, with no guessing missing source card/picture values. Step5 do not automatically certify student physical representation or oral explanation. Identify columns as vertical, rows horizontal in authentic source arrays; count total by equal columns and repeated addition.","Likely error: classify parity without checking leftover, confuse equal groups with arbitrary dots, call nonrectangular spacing an array, or use unequal-size partition tiles. Original printed source arrays and student rows/columns drawing human.","Grade2 Unit8 Lesson8. Source: Identify columns as vertical, rows horizontal in authentic source arrays; count total by equal columns and repeated addition. Human boundary: Original printed source arrays and student rows/columns drawing human."}),
new(129,"PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G2:U08:L09","https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-2/unit-8/lesson-9/preparation.html","bcb72af780ce091cc45149ffbe48c2e8b3bf7f65bd96ca98538d30d5ea880d61",new string[]{"CCSS:2.OA.B.2","CCSS:2.OA.C.3","CCSS:2.OA.C.4"},new string[]{"Learning goal: You will make connections between the structure of an array and expressions that represent the sum of the number of objects in each row or column in an array.\nIn this lesson, you match expressions with equal addends to arrays and find the total number of objects in an array by finding the value of these sums. The primary focus of the lesson is on relating sums with equal addends to the structure of the rows and columns in an array to build foundations for using arrays to represent multiplication in grade 3. For example, you might find the total number in the array by adding \\(6 + 6\\) .","Key ideas:\n- Represent the number of objects in an array as a sum of equal addends\n- In this lesson, you match expressions with equal addends to arrays and find the total number of objects in an array by finding the value of these sums\n- The addends are the quantities being combined and the sum is the resulting total; the expression must match the representation.","Example 1: How many waffles are on the tray? Record an estimate that is: too low about right too high \\(\\phantom{\\hspace{2.5cm} \\\\ \\hspace{2.5cm}}\\) \\(\\phantom{\\hspace{2.5cm} \\\\ \\hspace{2.5cm}}\\) \\(\\phantom{\\hspace{2.5cm} \\\\ \\hspace{2.5cm}}\\)\nApproach: “Even though we can’t see the whole tray, some you are very confident about your estimates. Why do you think that is?” (If the tray is full, it has 5 rows with 4 in each row. Even though we can’t see all the waffles, we know how many fit.)\nExample 2: Mai and Diego represented the same array with different expressions. Diego’s expression \\(2 + 2 + 2 + 2 + 2 +2\\) Mai’s expression \\(6 + 6\\) Who do you agree with? Show your thinking using diagrams, symbols, or other representations.\nApproach: “Which expression represents the sum of the number in each row? Explain.” ( \\(2 + 2 + 2 + 2 + 2 + 2\\) because there are 2 in each row.) “Mai’s expression is \\(6 + 6\\) . What are the different ways Mai may see \\(6 + 6\\) in this array?” (She may see 6 red and 6 yellow counters. She may see 2 rows with 6 counters in each row.)","Step 1: Identify the quantities being combined.\nStep 2: Represent each quantity with objects, a drawing, number line, or expression.\nStep 3: Write an addition relationship that matches the representation.\nStep 4: Find the total using an efficient strategy.\nStep 5: Check that the total matches the original quantities.","Common mistake: counting an item twice or combining the wrong quantities. Check the addends and verify the total.","You will make connections between the structure of an array and expressions that represent the sum of the number of objects in each row or column in an array. In this lesson, you match expressions with equal addends to arrays and find the total number of objects in an array by finding the value of these sums."},new string[]{"IM2021 G2 Unit8 Lesson9 A Sum of Equal Addends PLC Activity. Actual original publisher activity: Original waffle tray5rows×4=20 if complete; express row sums or column sums for true arrays of equal addends. OPEN source boundaries: Actual waffle picture partly hidden, row/column drawing and student explanations human.","Authentic lesson-specific goal: Original waffle tray5rows×4=20 if complete; express row sums or column sums for true arrays of equal addends. Source CCSS references CCSS:2.OA.B.2,CCSS:2.OA.C.3,CCSS:2.OA.C.4. FIRST-PARTY numerical practice is independently authored, not a publisher assessment.","Authentic student-facing activity, with source number constraints and teacher-only drawings: Original waffle tray5rows×4=20 if complete; express row sums or column sums for true arrays of equal addends. In Edulytics, use newly constructed numerical cases and disclose independent origin. Actual waffle picture partly hidden, row/column drawing and student explanations human.","Step1 identify actual publisher objective. Step2 show original with real counters, pair shares, equal addends, array or physical equal-size square tile. Step3 for independent first-party practice count equal groups, parity, repeated addition, or array size precisely. Step4 confirm residuals or row/column sums, with no guessing missing source card/picture values. Step5 do not automatically certify student physical representation or oral explanation. Original waffle tray5rows×4=20 if complete; express row sums or column sums for true arrays of equal addends.","Likely error: classify parity without checking leftover, confuse equal groups with arbitrary dots, call nonrectangular spacing an array, or use unequal-size partition tiles. Actual waffle picture partly hidden, row/column drawing and student explanations human.","Grade2 Unit8 Lesson9. Source: Original waffle tray5rows×4=20 if complete; express row sums or column sums for true arrays of equal addends. Human boundary: Actual waffle picture partly hidden, row/column drawing and student explanations human."}),
new(130,"PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G2:U08:L10","https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-2/unit-8/lesson-10/preparation.html","3e31c5d65eba1e6839a052f2d5e823e6e0d0229ae979fbcd8b7aec49c78615ed",new string[]{"CCSS:2.NBT.A.2","CCSS:2.OA.B.2","CCSS:2.OA.C.3","CCSS:2.OA.C.4"},new string[]{"Learning goal: You will write equations to show the sum of the rows or columns of an array.\nIn a previous lesson, you matched arrays to expressions with equal addends.","Key ideas:\n- Represent the number of objects in each column or in each row of an array using equal addend equations\n- In a previous lesson, you matched arrays to expressions with equal addends\n- The addends are the quantities being combined and the sum is the resulting total; the expression must match the representation.","Example 1: Decide if each statement is true or false. Be prepared to explain your reasoning. \\(2 + 2 + 2 = 3 + 3\\) \\(4 + 4 + 4 = 3 + 3 + 3 + 3\\) \\(5 + 5 + 5 = 3 + 3 + 3\\)\nApproach: “How could we make the last statement true based on this array? Explain.” (To make this true we need \\(3 + 3 + 3 + 3 + 3\\) . I know that if \\(5 + 5 + 5\\) means 3 columns of 5, there would be 5 rows of 3.)\nExample 2: Use 20 counters to make an array with 4 rows. How many columns does your array have? Fill in the blanks to create equations with equal addends that represent the array. \\(\\underline{\\hspace{1 cm}}+\\underline{\\hspace{1 cm}}+\\underline{\\hspace{1 cm}}+\\underline{\\hspace{1 cm}}=\\underline{\\hspace{1 cm}}\\) \\(\\underline{\\hspace{1 cm}}+\\underline{\\hspace{1...\nApproach: Represent the quantities, write a matching addition relationship, then combine or count on to find and check the total.","Step 1: Identify the quantities being combined.\nStep 2: Represent each quantity with objects, a drawing, number line, or expression.\nStep 3: Write an addition relationship that matches the representation.\nStep 4: Find the total using an efficient strategy.\nStep 5: Check that the total matches the original quantities.","Common mistake: counting an item twice or combining the wrong quantities. Check the addends and verify the total.","You will write equations to show the sum of the rows or columns of an array. In a previous lesson, you matched arrays to expressions with equal addends."},new string[]{"IM2021 G2 Unit8 Lesson10 Write Expressions and Equations to Represent Arrays. Actual original publisher activity: Source 5rows×3 array can be 3+3+3+3+3 or 5+5+5; student constructs counters and writes row and column equations. OPEN source boundaries: Original source images, constructed physical arrays and valid alternate written sums human.","Authentic lesson-specific goal: Source 5rows×3 array can be 3+3+3+3+3 or 5+5+5; student constructs counters and writes row and column equations. Source CCSS references CCSS:2.NBT.A.2,CCSS:2.OA.B.2,CCSS:2.OA.C.3,CCSS:2.OA.C.4. FIRST-PARTY numerical practice is independently authored, not a publisher assessment.","Authentic student-facing activity, with source number constraints and teacher-only drawings: Source 5rows×3 array can be 3+3+3+3+3 or 5+5+5; student constructs counters and writes row and column equations. In Edulytics, use newly constructed numerical cases and disclose independent origin. Original source images, constructed physical arrays and valid alternate written sums human.","Step1 identify actual publisher objective. Step2 show original with real counters, pair shares, equal addends, array or physical equal-size square tile. Step3 for independent first-party practice count equal groups, parity, repeated addition, or array size precisely. Step4 confirm residuals or row/column sums, with no guessing missing source card/picture values. Step5 do not automatically certify student physical representation or oral explanation. Source 5rows×3 array can be 3+3+3+3+3 or 5+5+5; student constructs counters and writes row and column equations.","Likely error: classify parity without checking leftover, confuse equal groups with arbitrary dots, call nonrectangular spacing an array, or use unequal-size partition tiles. Original source images, constructed physical arrays and valid alternate written sums human.","Grade2 Unit8 Lesson10. Source: Source 5rows×3 array can be 3+3+3+3+3 or 5+5+5; student constructs counters and writes row and column equations. Human boundary: Original source images, constructed physical arrays and valid alternate written sums human."}),
new(131,"PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G2:U08:L11","https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-2/unit-8/lesson-11/preparation.html","030aa078fcfa9ec220f64cee9e5c18b39fbeb418ef855764a35d9f31cd19910e",new string[]{"CCSS:2.G.A.2","CCSS:2.OA.C.3","CCSS:2.OA.C.4"},new string[]{"Learning goal: You will see that a rectangle partitioned into equal-size squares is composed of squares that are arranged in rows and columns.\nIn an earlier unit, you composed larger shapes from composite shapes and partitioned rectangles to make halves, thirds, or fourths. Although you have experience with partitioning, the focus for this work is different. You will not name the parts in terms of fractions, but you will attend to making equal-size squares.","Key ideas:\n- Create arrays using square tiles and partially-partitioned rectangles\n- In an earlier unit, you composed larger shapes from composite shapes and partitioned rectangles to make halves, thirds, or fourths\n- Use mathematical attributes such as sides, angles, parallelism, or length—not size or orientation—to justify a classification.","Example 1: Which one doesn’t belong? A Caption: A B Caption: B Attribution: By Congerdesign. Pixabay. Pixabay. Source . C Caption: C D Caption: D\nApproach: “What do B, C, and D have in common?” (You all show arrays with 3 in each row.)\nExample 2: Choose a number of tiles. 12 15 16 18 20 Arrange all the tiles in an array. Then push them together to make a rectangle. Shade in the same arrangement of squares on the grid paper. How many rows of squares does your rectangle have? ___ How many columns does your rectangle have? ___ How many tiles are in your rectangle? ___ Write 2 equations to represent...\nApproach: Identify the relevant attributes of the figure and use those attributes—not size or orientation—to justify the answer.","Step 1: Identify the figure and the attributes named in the task.\nStep 2: Measure, count, or compare sides, angles, corners, or other relevant features.\nStep 3: Use the attributes to classify, construct, or reason about the figure.\nStep 4: Explain which attributes support the result.\nStep 5: Check that the conclusion does not depend only on size or orientation.","Common mistake: classifying a figure by appearance, size, or orientation. Use its mathematical attributes instead.","You will see that a rectangle partitioned into equal-size squares is composed of squares that are arranged in rows and columns. In an earlier unit, you composed larger shapes from composite shapes and partitioned rectangles to make halves, thirds, or fourths."},new string[]{"IM2021 G2 Unit8 Lesson11 Arrays and Rectangles. Actual original publisher activity: Source rectangle D4rows×3 unit squares; construct tiled rectangles, equal-size squares no overlap or gap, identify rows and columns. OPEN source boundaries: Real unit square size, placement, original source rectangle figures and partner responses human.","Authentic lesson-specific goal: Source rectangle D4rows×3 unit squares; construct tiled rectangles, equal-size squares no overlap or gap, identify rows and columns. Source CCSS references CCSS:2.G.A.2,CCSS:2.OA.C.3,CCSS:2.OA.C.4. FIRST-PARTY numerical practice is independently authored, not a publisher assessment.","Authentic student-facing activity, with source number constraints and teacher-only drawings: Source rectangle D4rows×3 unit squares; construct tiled rectangles, equal-size squares no overlap or gap, identify rows and columns. In Edulytics, use newly constructed numerical cases and disclose independent origin. Real unit square size, placement, original source rectangle figures and partner responses human.","Step1 identify actual publisher objective. Step2 show original with real counters, pair shares, equal addends, array or physical equal-size square tile. Step3 for independent first-party practice count equal groups, parity, repeated addition, or array size precisely. Step4 confirm residuals or row/column sums, with no guessing missing source card/picture values. Step5 do not automatically certify student physical representation or oral explanation. Source rectangle D4rows×3 unit squares; construct tiled rectangles, equal-size squares no overlap or gap, identify rows and columns.","Likely error: classify parity without checking leftover, confuse equal groups with arbitrary dots, call nonrectangular spacing an array, or use unequal-size partition tiles. Real unit square size, placement, original source rectangle figures and partner responses human.","Grade2 Unit8 Lesson11. Source: Source rectangle D4rows×3 unit squares; construct tiled rectangles, equal-size squares no overlap or gap, identify rows and columns. Human boundary: Real unit square size, placement, original source rectangle figures and partner responses human."}),
new(132,"PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G2:U08:L12","https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-2/unit-8/lesson-12/preparation.html","c5b98eb205dbd29fc855ea6c8a4f31fe813ba9701e94f8d84e5300b398494883",new string[]{"CCSS:2.G.A.2","CCSS:2.OA.C.4"},new string[]{"Learning goal: You will partition rectangles into equal-size squares.\nYou have arranged tiles to make arrays and rectangles, represented your rectangles by shading squares on a grid, and completed the partitioning of rectangles into equal-size squares. In this lesson, you partition rectangles into equal-size squares with and without guiding marks and represent the total number of squares within the rectangles with equations that show the sum of the number of squares in each row or the number of squares in each column.","Key ideas:\n- Partition rectangles into rows and columns of equal-size squares, and count to find the total number of squares\n- You have arranged tiles to make arrays and rectangles, represented your rectangles by shading squares on a grid, and completed the partitioning of rectangles into equal-size squares\n- The addends are the quantities being combined and the sum is the resulting total; the expression must match the representation.","Example 1: How many little squares would fill the rectangle? Record an estimate that is: too low about right too high \\(\\phantom{\\hspace{2.5cm} \\\\ \\hspace{2.5cm}}\\) \\(\\phantom{\\hspace{2.5cm} \\\\ \\hspace{2.5cm}}\\) \\(\\phantom{\\hspace{2.5cm} \\\\ \\hspace{2.5cm}}\\)\nApproach: Represent both quantities, compare their values, and state the relationship using words, symbols, or a clear explanation.\nExample 2: Build a rectangle with 8 tiles arranged in 2 rows. Use a ruler to partition the rectangle to match the rectangle you made. Use a ruler to partition the rectangle using the tick marks as a guide. How many rows of equal-size squares did you make? How many columns did you make? Write 2 equations to represent the total number of equal-size squares. Use a...\nApproach: “How is this partition different from what we did in this activity?” (The squares aren’t the same size and the last column has 4 not 3.)","Step 1: Identify the quantities or expressions to compare.\nStep 2: Represent or calculate each value accurately.\nStep 3: Decide whether one is greater, less, or equal.\nStep 4: Write or explain the comparison.\nStep 5: Recheck both values before finalizing.","Common mistake: deciding from appearance instead of actual values. Represent or calculate both quantities before comparing.","You will partition rectangles into equal-size squares. You have arranged tiles to make arrays and rectangles, represented your rectangles by shading squares on a grid, and completed the partitioning of rectangles into equal-size squares."},new string[]{"IM2021 G2 Unit8 Lesson12 Partition Rectangles into Squares. Actual original publisher activity: Source partly-obscured square array estimation; partition physical rectangles into equal squares using ruler and inch tiles, then without guide ticks. OPEN source boundaries: Actual source rectangle partitions, measured equal-size square units, source visual gaps and drawing human.","Authentic lesson-specific goal: Source partly-obscured square array estimation; partition physical rectangles into equal squares using ruler and inch tiles, then without guide ticks. Source CCSS references CCSS:2.G.A.2,CCSS:2.OA.C.4. FIRST-PARTY numerical practice is independently authored, not a publisher assessment.","Authentic student-facing activity, with source number constraints and teacher-only drawings: Source partly-obscured square array estimation; partition physical rectangles into equal squares using ruler and inch tiles, then without guide ticks. In Edulytics, use newly constructed numerical cases and disclose independent origin. Actual source rectangle partitions, measured equal-size square units, source visual gaps and drawing human.","Step1 identify actual publisher objective. Step2 show original with real counters, pair shares, equal addends, array or physical equal-size square tile. Step3 for independent first-party practice count equal groups, parity, repeated addition, or array size precisely. Step4 confirm residuals or row/column sums, with no guessing missing source card/picture values. Step5 do not automatically certify student physical representation or oral explanation. Source partly-obscured square array estimation; partition physical rectangles into equal squares using ruler and inch tiles, then without guide ticks.","Likely error: classify parity without checking leftover, confuse equal groups with arbitrary dots, call nonrectangular spacing an array, or use unequal-size partition tiles. Actual source rectangle partitions, measured equal-size square units, source visual gaps and drawing human.","Grade2 Unit8 Lesson12. Source: Source partly-obscured square array estimation; partition physical rectangles into equal squares using ruler and inch tiles, then without guide ticks. Human boundary: Actual source rectangle partitions, measured equal-size square units, source visual gaps and drawing human."}),
new(133,"PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G2:U08:L13","https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-2/unit-8/lesson-13/preparation.html","0e2da35fe5896a5b9d33957631e30236394805f2619dea1fbbea3cb3fc6de2ab",new string[]{"CCSS:2.NBT.A.2","CCSS:2.NBT.B.7"},new string[]{"Learning goal: You will skip-count by 2, 5, and 10 and to add and subtract within 1,000.\nThis lesson is optional because it is an opportunity for extra practice that not all classes may need. In this lesson you have another chance to practice skip-counting by 2, 5, and 10 and addition and subtraction within 1,000.","Key ideas:\n- Add and subtract within 1,000 using strategies based on place value and the properties of operations\n- Skip-count by 2, 5, and 10\n- Subtraction can represent taking away, comparing quantities, or finding a missing addend; the representation must match the situation.","Example 1: Decide if each statement is true or false. Be prepared to explain your reasoning. \\(2 + 2 + 2 + 2 = 4 + 4\\) \\(2 + 2 + 2 = 3 + 3\\) \\(2 + 2 + 2 + 2 + 2 + 2 = 5 + 5\\)\nApproach: Represent the whole and the part, write a matching subtraction relationship, then find and check the difference.","Step 1: Identify the whole and the part being removed or compared.\nStep 2: Represent the situation with objects, a drawing, number line, or equation.\nStep 3: Write a subtraction relationship that matches the representation.\nStep 4: Find the difference using an efficient strategy.\nStep 5: Check the result with the original quantities or a related addition fact.","Common mistake: mixing up the whole and the part being removed or compared. Check that the subtraction relationship matches the representation.","You will skip-count by 2, 5, and 10 and to add and subtract within 1,000. This lesson is optional because it is an opportunity for extra practice that not all classes may need."},new string[]{"IM2021 G2 Unit8 Lesson13 Center Day 2 (optional). Actual original publisher activity: OPTIONAL true/false repeated 2s: four 2s=4+4 